7
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:34,520
If we are going to learn how to do chemistry, the first thing we need to do is learn a little

8
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:39,540
bit about all of the equipment in the laboratory. We are probably familiar with the way that

9
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:46,640
chemistry labs are portrayed in movies, cartoons, and horrible stock images. But not surprisingly,

10
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:52,120
a real chemistry lab does not look very much like this. Instead, a chemistry laboratory

11
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:57,000
will typically look something like this. So let's familiarize ourselves with the

12
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,760
instruments we will be using on a regular basis. First off, if we are going to do chemistry,

13
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:08,040
we are going to have to use lots of glassware. Let's start with one of the more familiar items.

14
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:15,000
These are beakers. There is a range of beaker sizes available, ranging from as small as 5

15
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,360
milliliters to as large as several liters, and there are different situations in which these

16
00:01:20,360 --> 00:01:26,760
different sizes would be desirable. Most often we tend to use beakers between around 10 to 400

17
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:33,640
milliliters. Beakers have many uses. Reactions can be conducted in them. They can be used to

18
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:39,960
transport solutions and solvents. Or they can be used as water baths, meaning we can suspend

19
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:46,120
some other vessel in the beaker in order to control its temperature. Always be aware of what

20
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:51,320
you are using a beaker for, and use the appropriately sized beaker for the job.

21
00:01:51,320 --> 00:01:56,040
The measurements on the sides are great for estimating the volume, but we do not carry out

22
00:01:56,040 --> 00:02:01,240
actual measurements of volume using beakers. We have glassware that is more accurate for measuring

23
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:07,720
volumes when necessary. Speaking of measuring volumes, next up we have the graduated cylinder.

24
00:02:07,720 --> 00:02:13,400
A graduated cylinder is used to measure the volume of a liquid. These also range quite a

25
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:19,000
bit in size. Be aware that they are tall, so you may at some point accidentally knock one over.

26
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:24,040
In order to avoid this, always place them down where you can see them or where less movement is

27
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:28,920
occurring. We should also be aware that when taking measurements with a graduated cylinder,

28
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:34,680
we will always read from the bottom of this concave meniscus. This word refers to the way

29
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:39,960
the liquid climbs a little bit up the edges, and to avoid ambiguity, we will take readings

30
00:02:39,960 --> 00:02:47,720
from this position here. Next we have test tubes, test tube racks, and test tube holders.

31
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:54,280
A test tube is a very small reaction vessel which is nice to use when observing obvious visible

32
00:02:54,280 --> 00:02:59,400
changes in the reaction. They don't stand up by themselves, so be sure to place them in a test

33
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:05,240
tube rack so they don't roll away. A test tube holder, like this one, can be used to hold a

34
00:03:05,240 --> 00:03:11,480
test tube above the flame of a Bunsen burner or suspend it in a water bath. Get comfortable

35
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:16,680
holding one of these before heating it, and be sure to have one that fits the size of the test tube

36
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:22,600
you are using. Next, let's check out another recognizable piece of glassware, the Erlenmeyer

37
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:29,080
flask. A conical, or Erlenmeyer flask, looks like this. These are typically used for mixing

38
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:34,280
reagents together and conducting a reaction. Again, they come in various sizes, and the

39
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:39,960
measurements on the sides are quite imprecise, so they shouldn't be used when accuracy is necessary.

40
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:46,520
The tapered edges leading to the small opening at the top prevent spilling, and they also make these

41
00:03:46,520 --> 00:03:52,200
flasks ideal for clamping, which is something we will commonly want to do. If the mixture needs

42
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:57,400
to be manually stirred, you can manipulate them with less fear of the solution escaping,

43
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:01,160
which is why these are typically preferred for running reactions over beakers.

44
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:08,040
Next we have the indispensable Buechner flask, or filter flask. These are very similar to the

45
00:04:08,040 --> 00:04:14,280
Erlenmeyer flask. It is simply that the Buechner flask has a side arm which is used when conducting

46
00:04:14,280 --> 00:04:19,720
vacuum filtration, which we will discuss in another tutorial. Avoid using these for anything

47
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:24,600
other than filtrations, as the side arm opening can cause the liquid to escape.

48
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:31,880
With the Buechner flask covered, let's check out the Buechner funnel and accompanying rubber

49
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:38,680
adapter. The Buechner funnel is also used when conducting vacuum filtrations. These sit in the

50
00:04:38,680 --> 00:04:45,320
Buechner flask, and are typically made of ceramic, but plastic ones are sometimes used. For a better

51
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:50,520
seal, we tend to use the rubber adapter. These aren't all that stable, so be sure to clamp

52
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:57,320
the flask first before assembling the filtration setup. Next let's examine the filter funnel.

53
00:04:57,320 --> 00:05:03,000
For gravity filtrations we use this kind of funnel. Either cotton wool or a folded piece

54
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:07,800
of filter paper can be used for gravity filtrations, which will also be properly

55
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:13,240
demonstrated later. To finish off the funnels, let's check out the powder funnel.

56
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:19,240
When wanting to add powder to a flask, which could be a drying agent or any solid reagent,

57
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:25,160
sometimes it is much easier to use a powder funnel. These can be placed on top of a flask,

58
00:05:25,160 --> 00:05:30,520
and a solid can be added easily. These typically are not made of glass, so they are easily

59
00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:36,440
distinguishable from the other funnels. Be sure to check and see if it is clean before using it,

60
00:05:36,440 --> 00:05:44,280
and clean it afterwards. Next we have the spatula. When picking up solids, we will use a spatula.

61
00:05:44,280 --> 00:05:50,200
Some are curved, some are flat, and they usually bend if necessary, but be gentle whenever possible.

62
00:05:51,160 --> 00:05:57,320
Similarly we have the stirring rod. When wanting to briefly stir something, a stirring rod is the

63
00:05:57,320 --> 00:06:02,440
best option. Do not get this confused with a thermometer, and be careful when putting these

64
00:06:02,440 --> 00:06:08,520
down as they do roll away. Then we get to the pipette. For adding small amounts of liquid,

65
00:06:08,520 --> 00:06:14,840
we use a pipette. Using a pipette bulb, a liquid can be taken up and put somewhere else.

66
00:06:14,840 --> 00:06:20,600
Simply squeeze, place into the liquid, release the bulb to draw the liquid into the pipette,

67
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:25,960
and then transfer it to the new vessel. Next up we have the burette. These are

68
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:31,960
commonly used with titrations. Like a graduated cylinder it has these gradations for measuring

69
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:37,560
volume, but unlike a graduated cylinder they are inverted, so that you can measure how much

70
00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:42,680
volume is being added to some other vessel. A more thorough demonstration of this instrument

71
00:06:42,680 --> 00:06:48,680
will be shown when we conduct a titration. Then there is the volumetric flask. When

72
00:06:48,680 --> 00:06:55,080
making standard solutions we use a volumetric flask. Much like the volumetric pipette,

73
00:06:55,080 --> 00:07:00,600
a single line indicates the exact volume of this flask. Because of the long necks,

74
00:07:00,600 --> 00:07:05,480
these can be knocked over, so like the graduated cylinders, be careful where you place them.

75
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:11,480
Also check which cap you have, as some fit nicely, but others are deceptive. When using

76
00:07:11,480 --> 00:07:16,440
these to make solutions, be extra careful once you reach the neck, as this will fill up much

77
00:07:16,440 --> 00:07:22,440
more rapidly than the bulb, as it is so narrow. Moving along we find the separatory funnel,

78
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:27,000
which we learned about when going over extraction in the organic chemistry series.

79
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:31,560
For separating two immiscible liquids, we will use a separatory funnel,

80
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:38,040
sometimes abbreviated as a sep funnel. When using these, make sure you have the correct size cap,

81
00:07:38,040 --> 00:07:44,680
so that nothing spills when inverted. Sepphunnels require a ring clamp, so make sure that the size

82
00:07:44,680 --> 00:07:50,520
of your ring clamp is appropriate, as if too small the funnel will be unstable, but if too

83
00:07:50,520 --> 00:07:56,040
big it will not hold the funnel at all. We will notice that the separatory funnel has a valve

84
00:07:56,120 --> 00:08:02,040
for releasing liquid. Sometimes the valve comes assembled, and sometimes they need to be assembled.

85
00:08:02,040 --> 00:08:05,720
We will talk more about this apparatus when we perform an extraction.

86
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:12,200
Now this is called a dropping funnel. A dropping funnel looks very similar to a sep funnel,

87
00:08:12,200 --> 00:08:17,880
but they have this separate arm and a ground glass joint. These joints become important when

88
00:08:17,880 --> 00:08:23,800
conducting reactions under nitrogen or argon atmosphere with air sensitive reagents and

89
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:29,640
products. They are used to control the addition of a liquid to a reaction, and the arm keeps the

90
00:08:29,640 --> 00:08:34,840
pressure constant, so the addition is smooth and continuous. If there is no need for the ground

91
00:08:34,840 --> 00:08:39,960
glass joint, a sep funnel will often suffice as a dropping funnel, but it is good to know

92
00:08:39,960 --> 00:08:45,400
what this is if you come across one. Next we find the mortar and pestle.

93
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:50,920
If you need to crush up some solid material into a powder, you'd use a mortar and pestle.

94
00:08:50,920 --> 00:08:55,240
These are pretty simple, you simply put the material in the mortar and crush it with the

95
00:08:55,240 --> 00:08:59,560
pestle. These are usually ceramic and quite heavy, and they are fun to use.

96
00:09:00,280 --> 00:09:04,680
Now we get to that ubiquitous piece of glassware for the organic chemist,

97
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:10,440
the round bottom flask. As we will come to see, for more serious reactions we will use a round

98
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:16,040
bottom flask. There are many sizes, and they almost always have at least one ground glass

99
00:09:16,040 --> 00:09:21,880
joint in them. The roundness allows for better heat distribution and stirring, where the joints

100
00:09:21,880 --> 00:09:27,640
allow for easy fitting with other glassware. Since they don't stand on their own, we will use a cork

101
00:09:27,640 --> 00:09:33,720
ring to hold them up, which must be of an appropriate size. If securing to a solid support,

102
00:09:33,720 --> 00:09:39,000
be sure to clamp them around the ground glass joint area, as this is where the glass is the

103
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:45,000
toughest. To be clear, this goes for all glassware. The ground glass joint is the thickest part,

104
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:52,360
and should be the area clamped whenever possible. Moving along, this is a reflux condenser. When we

105
00:09:52,360 --> 00:09:58,440
want to boil a liquid but don't want to lose any of the volume, we will use a reflux condenser.

106
00:09:58,440 --> 00:10:03,240
These can look very different from one another, but they are all used the same way. They are

107
00:10:03,240 --> 00:10:09,240
essentially a tube with a jacket, although some are just a tube, but these are less reliable.

108
00:10:09,880 --> 00:10:16,280
The glass jacket is filled with water through these side arms which cools the environment in the tube,

109
00:10:16,280 --> 00:10:22,360
allowing for the gas to condense and drip back into the solution. In this way, the liquid is

110
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:28,760
always boiling but never escapes the reaction vessel. Tubes are attached to the arms here,

111
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:34,040
with water flowing in from the bottom and out the top through a separate tube. It is very

112
00:10:34,040 --> 00:10:38,440
important to mention that we must never block the top of the condenser with anything,

113
00:10:38,440 --> 00:10:42,840
as this will cause the pressure to build up, which could result in an accident.

114
00:10:42,840 --> 00:10:46,600
A more thorough demonstration of this apparatus will be conducted later.

115
00:10:47,480 --> 00:10:52,840
Here we have some clamps and a clamp stand, something we've already seen a few times by now.

116
00:10:52,840 --> 00:10:58,120
As you have likely gathered, these are used to hold up glassware and keep them locked in place.

117
00:10:58,760 --> 00:11:03,000
When using these, be careful with the weight distribution, as we don't want the clamp

118
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:07,960
stand to topple over. The base may jut out in one direction more than another,

119
00:11:07,960 --> 00:11:13,480
so don't overload one side when possible. Clamps themselves are very adjustable with

120
00:11:13,480 --> 00:11:18,840
several screws, and there are a few different styles in terms of precisely how they grip the

121
00:11:18,840 --> 00:11:24,760
glassware. Familiarize yourself with these, as we should know what kind of clamp will work best

122
00:11:24,760 --> 00:11:31,320
with a particular piece of glassware. Now here, this lovely instrument is a column.

123
00:11:31,320 --> 00:11:36,200
We use these when performing column chromatography. They are essentially big

124
00:11:36,200 --> 00:11:42,040
long glass tubes with a valve at the bottom. Some have a ground glass joint at the top,

125
00:11:42,040 --> 00:11:48,440
and some have solvent reservoirs. Some have a frit at the bottom which is a porous material,

126
00:11:48,440 --> 00:11:54,040
and some don't. We will learn how to assemble and use one of these in a later tutorial,

127
00:11:54,040 --> 00:11:59,960
but for now we know what they look like. Now getting to some of the smaller accoutrement,

128
00:11:59,960 --> 00:12:06,120
this is filter paper. Filter paper comes in various sizes and gradings depending on the

129
00:12:06,120 --> 00:12:12,040
size of the particles you are filtering. Generally speaking, a finer grading will filter a solution

130
00:12:12,040 --> 00:12:18,520
more slowly. Filter paper will be used for the Buechner filtration setup, but can also be used

131
00:12:18,520 --> 00:12:24,280
for gravity filtrations, which we will perform later in the series. Filter paper can also be

132
00:12:24,280 --> 00:12:31,480
used to passively dry solids as they tend to absorb liquids. Now this is a watch glass.

133
00:12:32,040 --> 00:12:38,280
A watch glass is just a simple piece of glassware that can be used to hold and weigh an amount of

134
00:12:38,280 --> 00:12:45,720
solid. They wobble a bit, so be gentle with them. Similarly, this is an evaporating dish.

135
00:12:45,720 --> 00:12:52,120
Sometimes you may need to let a wet solid sit and dry. We can use an evaporating dish for this.

136
00:12:52,120 --> 00:12:58,760
They are typically ceramic and have high edges. Now this little thing is a melting point tube.

137
00:12:58,760 --> 00:13:04,600
When performing melting point determination, you need a melting point tube. These look like little

138
00:13:04,600 --> 00:13:09,640
capillaries, but they are in fact sealed at one end. They are very thin and small,

139
00:13:09,640 --> 00:13:15,160
so make sure you have an area where you can safely place them. Solid is added to the open

140
00:13:15,160 --> 00:13:21,080
end, and the tube can be placed in any melting point apparatus when measuring the melting point

141
00:13:21,080 --> 00:13:28,680
of a compound. In the same vein, here is an NMR tube and cap. When you need to obtain an NMR

142
00:13:28,680 --> 00:13:35,400
spectrum, this is the tube we will use, and here is the cap we use. Simply make a rather concentrated

143
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:40,920
solution of the compound you'd like to run, add it to the tube with a pipette, and then cap it.

144
00:13:40,920 --> 00:13:46,280
This is now ready for the spectrometer. Now let's head over to the fume hood.

145
00:13:46,280 --> 00:13:51,400
We will tend to run reactions in here, so many instruments will live inside the hood.

146
00:13:51,960 --> 00:13:57,720
This is a combination hot plate and magnetic stirrer. As one would guess, this piece of

147
00:13:57,720 --> 00:14:04,120
equipment can both heat and stir your reaction. One dial will control the heat, and the other

148
00:14:04,120 --> 00:14:10,680
will control the rotational speed of the magnetic stir bar. The heating may need to be calibrated,

149
00:14:10,680 --> 00:14:17,080
as the dial may not be totally accurate. Some have a plug for an external thermometer to be used,

150
00:14:17,080 --> 00:14:23,560
so keep an eye out for that. As for the stirrer, a small Teflon stir bar can be placed in the

151
00:14:23,560 --> 00:14:30,040
solution, and the contents of the reaction flask will be mixed, like so. The stirring can get

152
00:14:30,040 --> 00:14:34,840
quite aggressive, so be sure to clamp up the vessel to stop it from possibly falling over.

153
00:14:35,720 --> 00:14:41,640
Another indispensable piece of equipment is the scale, or balance. Scales are used to weigh

154
00:14:41,640 --> 00:14:47,800
the mass of solids accurately. Be sure to tear the scale with any vessel you are weighing.

155
00:14:47,800 --> 00:14:53,000
What this means is that whatever will hold the solid, we can place it on the balance and zero

156
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:58,440
the mass, so that the measurement we take correlates precisely with the solid that is added.

157
00:14:59,080 --> 00:15:04,840
Make sure to avoid pouring or scooping the solid onto the vessel while it is on the scales,

158
00:15:04,840 --> 00:15:11,320
as any residual material may cause the expensive scales to break or malfunction. Take the vessel

159
00:15:11,320 --> 00:15:15,960
off the scale each time you want to add or remove solid from the vessel you are using.

160
00:15:16,920 --> 00:15:21,720
And that wraps up an introduction to the equipment you are most likely to use

161
00:15:21,720 --> 00:15:26,120
in the chemistry laboratory. Knowing how to use this equipment properly

162
00:15:26,200 --> 00:15:32,600
is the best way to stay safe while doing chemistry, in addition to becoming familiar with any eye wash

163
00:15:32,600 --> 00:15:39,480
or shower stations and appropriate disposal containers. These will be specific to your lab,

164
00:15:39,480 --> 00:15:44,680
so be sure to pay attention when instructed on how to use these by your own instructor.

165
00:15:44,680 --> 00:15:49,960
But with this basic information understood, let's move forward and start learning some simple

166
00:15:49,960 --> 00:15:51,960
techniques.
